# <APP>-SPEC-001 — <Application> product specification

## Context and intended outcome

Describe the user problem, evidence, desired outcome, and measurable signals. Distinguish known evidence from assumptions.

## Actors and permissions

Identify user, operator, administrator, external-system, and agent roles that apply. State authorization boundaries.

## Scope and exclusions

List included capabilities. List likely scope expansion and explicit exclusions with rationale.

## Functional requirements

Use a Markdown table with the columns `ID`, `Requirement`, and `Acceptance evidence`. Assign stable `FR-...` IDs. A requirement statement MUST contain at least one uppercase RFC 2119 keyword: `MUST`, `MUST NOT`, `REQUIRED`, `SHALL`, `SHALL NOT`, `SHOULD`, `SHOULD NOT`, `RECOMMENDED`, `MAY`, or `OPTIONAL`. Include actor, trigger, behavior, success, failure/empty/partial/unknown states, and observable acceptance evidence. The copied artifact helper checks table syntax and keyword casing in this section; it does not judge semantic completeness.

| ID | Requirement | Acceptance evidence |
| --- | --- | --- |

## Data and domain contracts

Define important entities, ownership, lifecycle, provenance, retention/deletion, validation, and source-of-truth rules. Record open data questions.

## Integrations and external boundaries

For each provider/system, state purpose, data exchanged, permissions, failure behavior, access status, and unresolved legal or commercial constraints. Do not imply an integration exists before evidence.

## Quality requirements

Use a Markdown table with the columns `ID`, `Requirement`, and `Acceptance evidence`. Assign stable `NFR-...` IDs. A requirement statement MUST contain at least one uppercase RFC 2119 keyword. Define applicability and observable evidence for security, privacy, accessibility, performance, reliability, observability, cost, localization, and operations. The copied artifact helper checks table syntax and keyword casing in this section; it does not judge semantic completeness.

| ID | Requirement | Acceptance evidence |
| --- | --- | --- |

## Acceptance outcomes

Define product-level outcomes and how they will be measured. Separate app behavior acceptance from business outcome validation.

## Assumptions and dependencies

Label material assumptions with stable IDs; state source type, confidence and limits, impact if wrong, confirmation/disproof methods, owner, due/review dates, affected requirements/tasks, blocking state, and disposition. Keep decision authority in the canonical decision log. Use the optional versioned assumption/experiment bundle and validator only when falsifiable experiments or expiry tracking are needed; an open, overdue, or unsupported assumption MUST NOT silently support a gate PASS.

## Open decision vectors

Use stable IDs (`D-...`). For each: unresolved question, viable options, impact, affected requirements/tasks, owner, and target decision point.

## Traceability

Link to `constitution.md`, `design.md`, `backlog.md`, decision log, research, and review records. Do not duplicate canonical content maintained in those artifacts.
